	"_comment": "Finnish (fi). Register matches real Finnish software UI text: the done/past slot uses the passive past participle (Tiedosto poistettu), the progress/gerund slot uses the passive present (Poistetaan...) — this IS the standard Finnish UI convention for status text, not a schema workaround. No articles, no grammatical gender (both correctly omitted per Finnish grammar). DESIGN DECISION — the noun 'other' slot carries the PARTITIVE SINGULAR, not the nominative plural: Finnish numerals govern the partitive singular (3 virhettä, never *3 virheet), and in an article-less locale the count path is the only consumer of 'other' (no plural articles and no suffixed definites exist to reuse the slot), so 'other' is free to carry the numeral-governed form; 'one' stays nominative singular, which is exactly what the numeral 1 governs (1 tiedosto). Two bridge entries carry lower confidence for GIT-SPECIFIC usage though the grammar itself is solid: 'commit' bridges to tallentaa (the general native verb for save/commit — Finnish git tooling vocabulary is unsettled and loanword-heavy in practice, and tallentaa/tallennettu is what most localised save-type UIs genuinely show); 'push' bridges to työntää (the literal, correct Finnish verb for a physical push — grammatically certain, but whether real Finnish git tooling actually uses this word for 'git push' specifically, versus keeping the English loanword, is less certain). Residual case limitation, now confined to fail.*: 'Ei voitu työntää haara' renders the object in the nominative ('one' form) where Finnish negation properly wants the partitive (haaraa) — the subject slot is shared with positions that need the nominative, so it cannot carry case; the count path, where the partitive is obligatory, is correct. 'item' -> kohde (target/item) is a defensible but not uniquely-correct rendering of a genuinely abstract English word.",
